body:
@petebacondarwin maybe you can explain a bit why this would solve my problem? My understanding is that ngcc is creating a lock file to avoid running multiple compilations in parallel in using the same node_modules directory, which is _exactly_ what I am trying to do. I presume that ngcc will be used when I compile my library. So how does doing this precompile step avoid the problem of multiple ngcc processes attempting to create the same lock file?

One extra piece of information: My pipeline's test stage is trying to run unit tests and e2e tests in parallel as two separate jobs. I see failures when those two jobs run in parallel.

And to be clear, the above suggestion did not resolve the issue. I still see failures about the lock file even after adding the postinstall ngcc step.

@elesueur ngcc does indeed prevent multiple instances from operating on the same node_modules directory, as that will result in duplicate processing which results in errors. That's why the lock file exists, which should typically allows ngcc to wait for other instances and pick up work if still necessary. In your case, this doesn't work out due to running the jobs in isolated containers, while operating on a shared `node_modules`. That means that there's no signalling possible between the various ngcc instances, which results in the error you're experiencing.

The proposal by @petebacondarwin is to run ngcc during the setup phase in a postinstall script. This should then process all of `node_modules` before spawning the individual jobs, which should allow the individual jobs to operate without needing additional ngcc processing (ngcc will only request a lock if it needs to do some work).

What could be the case is that the initial run of ngcc does not process all library bundles that are later requested by the individual jobs, in which case some jobs might still request the lock and find that's already taken. This may be due to the `--first-only` flag, which means that ngcc will stop processing once the first bundle format that was specified in `properties` has been processed. You could try removing that flag to see if it makes a difference, although it does make processing more expensive (as it will not longer skip several bundles). Unrelated: please do update to 10.1.2 as you'll find that ngcc runs much quicker.
